// Practice Test 2 — Scenarios: Developer Productivity (4), Claude Code for CI (5), Customer Support (1), Multi-Agent Research (3)
window.EXAMS = window.EXAMS || [];
window.EXAMS.push({
  id: 2,
  title: "Practice Test 2",
  questions: [
// ---------------- Scenario 4: Developer Productivity with Claude ----------------
{s:4,d:2,q:"An engineer asks the agent: “Find every place that calls calculateTax() so I know what breaks if I change its signature.” Which built-in tool should the agent use first?",
o:["Grep, because it searches file contents for a pattern such as the function name across every file in the repo",
"Glob, because it matches file paths by pattern and can locate every file whose name includes calculateTax in it",
"Read, because loading every file in the repository fully is the only way to guarantee that no callers are missed",
"Bash with ls -R, because listing the directory tree shows which modules are likely to call the tax function"],
a:[0],e:"Grep searches file contents for patterns like function names, error messages, or imports—exactly what finding callers requires. Glob matches file paths/names, not contents; reading every file is wasteful; directory listings don't reveal call sites."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"The agent needs to find all React test files, which follow the naming pattern *.test.tsx and are scattered across many directories. Which approach is most appropriate?",
o:["Glob with the pattern **/*.test.tsx, since it matches file paths by name pattern in every single directory",
"Grep for the string describe( across the repository, since every test file should contain at least one block",
"Read the package.json test script to find the directory where the test files are configured to be placed",
"Bash with cat on each folder in turn, reading the files and checking whether each one appears to be a test"],
a:[0],e:"Glob is for file path pattern matching (finding files by name or extension, e.g., **/*.test.tsx). Grep searches contents, which is indirect and could match non-test files; the other approaches are slow or unreliable."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"The agent tries to change a config value with Edit, but the call fails because the anchor text “timeout: 30” appears in four places in the file. What is the reliable fallback described for this situation?",
o:["Read the full file, then use Write to save the complete modified content, since Edit needs a unique text match",
"Retry the same Edit call repeatedly, since Edit picks a different occurrence of the matching text on each attempt",
"Use Grep with a replace flag to change every occurrence of the text across the file in one single operation",
"Use Glob to locate the file again, since a non-unique match error means the file path was resolved incorrectly"],
a:[0],e:"Edit performs targeted modifications using unique text matching. When the anchor isn't unique, Read + Write is the reliable fallback: load the full file, then write the complete modified content. Grep is search-only; the error isn't about the path."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"An engineer asks the agent to explain how authentication works in a large, unfamiliar monorepo. What exploration strategy is most effective?",
o:["Grep for entry points first, then Read the relevant files and follow imports to trace the flow across modules",
"Read every file in the repository upfront so the agent has the complete picture before answering any question",
"Glob for every file in the repository and summarize each one, building a complete index before investigation",
"Ask the user to explain the architecture first, since the agent cannot understand a codebase on its own anyway"],
a:[0],e:"Build understanding incrementally: start with Grep to find entry points, then Read to follow imports and trace flows—rather than reading everything upfront, which wastes context and dilutes attention."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"The agent searches for usages of formatCurrency() and finds only two. You know it's used widely—a utils/index.ts wrapper re-exports it as both money() and fmtPrice(). How should the agent trace all usages?",
o:["Identify every name the wrapper module exports the function under, then Grep the repo for each of those names",
"Grep only for the original function name with a case-insensitive flag, which also catches the renamed exports",
"Glob for files with the wrapper module's name, since any file that imports it must be named after the wrapper",
"Read the wrapper module alone, since it contains the complete list of every file that calls the wrapped function"],
a:[0],e:"To trace usage across wrapper modules, first identify all exported names (aliases), then search for each name across the codebase. Case-insensitivity doesn't catch different names; the wrapper doesn't know its callers."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"Your team built an MCP code_search tool backed by a semantic index that understands call graphs. Its description is “Searches code.” The agent almost always uses built-in Grep instead. What is the most effective first step?",
o:["Expand the MCP tool's description to explain its capabilities and outputs, and when it beats a plain text search",
"Remove Grep from the agent entirely, so that it has no choice but to use the MCP search tool for every lookup",
"Rename the MCP tool to grep so that the agent selects it whenever it would otherwise have chosen the built-in tool",
"Add a system prompt line saying the MCP tool exists, without changing the description provided by the server"],
a:[0],e:"Tool descriptions are the primary mechanism for selection. Enhancing MCP tool descriptions to explain capabilities and outputs keeps the agent from preferring built-in tools like Grep. Removing Grep loses a useful tool; renaming creates confusion."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"You want every developer on the team to get the GitHub MCP server automatically when they clone the repo, without committing anyone's personal access token. What is the right configuration?",
o:["Add the server to the project's .mcp.json and reference the token as ${GITHUB_TOKEN} so each developer uses their own",
"Add the server to the project's .mcp.json with the token hardcoded, and mark the repository private for protection",
"Ask each developer to add the server to their own ~/.claude.json, and share the token in the team chat channel",
"Commit the token in a .env file next to .mcp.json, since Claude Code reads .env files automatically for servers"],
a:[0],e:"Project-level .mcp.json is for shared team tooling and supports environment variable expansion (${GITHUB_TOKEN}), so credentials stay out of version control. Hardcoding or committing secrets is unsafe; per-user setup defeats sharing."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"You want to try out an experimental MCP server for your personal note-taking app. Teammates shouldn't see or load it. Where should you configure it?",
o:["In user-scoped configuration in ~/.claude.json, so it is available to you without affecting your teammates",
"In the project's .mcp.json on a separate branch, so that it is only loaded when that branch is checked out locally",
"In the project's CLAUDE.md as an @import, which registers the server only for whoever is running the session",
"In .claude/commands/ as a slash command file, which starts the server on demand whenever the command is invoked"],
a:[0],e:"Personal/experimental MCP servers belong in user scope (~/.claude.json). Branch tricks are fragile and still shareable; @import is for memory files; commands don't register MCP servers."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"A developer has the GitHub MCP server configured in the project's .mcp.json and a personal Notion MCP server in ~/.claude.json. What happens when Claude Code starts?",
o:["Tools from both servers are discovered at connection time and become available to the agent at the same time",
"Only the project-scoped server loads, since the project configuration overrides any servers in the user scope",
"Only the user-scoped server loads, since personal configuration always takes precedence over project settings",
"The agent must switch between the servers with a command, since only one MCP server can be active at a time"],
a:[0],e:"Tools from all configured MCP servers are discovered at connection time and are available simultaneously. Scopes determine where servers are configured and shared, not mutual exclusion."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"Before writing any SQL, the agent makes 10–15 exploratory tool calls listing tables and describing columns in your database MCP server. How can you reduce this exploration overhead?",
o:["Expose the database schema catalog as an MCP resource so the agent sees what data exists without exploring",
"Add a list_tables tool and instruct the agent to call it before each query, so it always has the latest schema",
"Paste the entire database schema into the system prompt of every session, regardless of which task is being done",
"Limit the agent to five tool calls per task so that it is forced to guess the table names rather than explore"],
a:[0],e:"MCP resources expose content catalogs (database schemas, documentation hierarchies, issue summaries) so agents can see what's available without exploratory tool calls. Another list tool still requires calls; pasting everything everywhere wastes context."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"Your team wants the agent to read and update Jira tickets. A well-maintained community Jira MCP server exists. What is the recommended approach?",
o:["Use an existing community Jira MCP server, and reserve custom servers for workflows that are specific to your team",
"Build a custom Jira MCP server from scratch, since community servers cannot be configured for a specific project",
"Skip MCP and have the agent call the Jira REST API through Bash with curl commands that are written in the prompt",
"Build a custom server that wraps the community server, so the team owns every line of the integration code path"],
a:[0],e:"Choose existing community MCP servers for standard integrations like Jira; reserve custom implementations for team-specific workflows. Building from scratch or wrapping adds maintenance without benefit; raw curl loses tool structure."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"Your developer-productivity agent has 18 tools covering git, CI, docs, tickets, databases, and file operations. It increasingly picks the wrong tool. What is the most effective improvement?",
o:["Scope the agent to the four or five tools its role needs, since large tool sets degrade the reliability of selection",
"Keep all 18 tools but sort them alphabetically, since the model reviews tools in order and stops at a good match",
"Add 18 few-shot examples, one for each tool, so that the model sees a demonstration of when every tool should be used",
"Raise max_tokens so the model has enough room to reason through all 18 tool options before it decides on its choice"],
a:[0],e:"Giving an agent too many tools (18 vs. 4–5) increases decision complexity and degrades selection. Scope tools by role (possibly splitting into specialized subagents). Ordering doesn't drive selection; max_tokens limits output length."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"The agent has a generic fetch_url tool for reading internal documentation, but it sometimes fetches arbitrary external sites, producing irrelevant or unsafe results. What is the best fix?",
o:["Replace fetch_url with a load_document tool that only accepts validated URLs for approved documentation",
"Keep fetch_url but add a prompt instruction asking the agent to only fetch URLs that point at documentation pages",
"Remove the URL-fetching ability entirely and require users to paste documentation into the chat themselves instead",
"Keep fetch_url and add a PostToolUse hook that deletes non-documentation results after they have been fetched"],
a:[0],e:"Replace generic tools with constrained alternatives (e.g., load_document that validates document URLs). The constraint lives in the tool contract, not in a probabilistic prompt. Post-fetch deletion still performs the unsafe fetch."},

{s:4,d:5,q:"Two hours into exploring a legacy codebase, the agent starts giving inconsistent answers and says things like “this typically uses a repository pattern” instead of naming the specific classes it found earlier. What helps most?",
o:["Have the agent record key findings in a scratchpad file and consult that file when answering later questions",
"Ask the agent to try harder to remember earlier findings, and remind it that typical patterns are not acceptable",
"Increase the temperature so the agent explores more varied answers rather than repeating generic typical patterns",
"Restart the session every few questions and have the agent rediscover the whole codebase from scratch each time"],
a:[0],e:"These are classic context-degradation symptoms in extended sessions. Scratchpad files persist key findings across context boundaries, and the agent consults them for later questions. Exhortation doesn't restore lost context; constant rediscovery is wasteful."},

{s:4,d:5,q:"Midway through a long investigation session in Claude Code, the context is filling with verbose Grep and Read output from the discovery phase. You want to keep working in this session. What should you do?",
o:["Run /compact to summarize the conversation and reduce context usage while preserving the important points so far",
"Run /memory to move the current conversation into CLAUDE.md so it no longer counts against the context window",
"Run /clear and continue the task, since Claude Code automatically restores the important findings after clearing",
"Run /init to regenerate CLAUDE.md, which automatically drops verbose tool output from the current context window"],
a:[0],e:"/compact reduces context usage during extended sessions when context fills with verbose discovery output. /clear wipes the conversation without preserving findings; /memory manages memory files; /init generates a CLAUDE.md."},

// ---------------- Scenario 5: Claude Code for Continuous Integration ----------------
{s:5,d:3,q:"Your CI job needs Claude Code to produce review findings that a script can post as inline PR comments with file, line, and severity. Which invocation is most appropriate?",
o:["claude -p with --output-format json and --json-schema, so findings are machine-parseable for inline PR comments",
"claude -p with a prompt asking for JSON, and a regex step that extracts the JSON block from the plain text output",
"claude in interactive mode with --verbose, and a script that scrapes the terminal output for any review findings",
"claude -p with --output-format text, and a second Claude call that converts the text findings into JSON afterward"],
a:[0],e:"-p runs non-interactively; --output-format json with --json-schema produces machine-parseable structured findings that conform to your schema, ready for automated posting. Regex extraction and scraping are brittle; a second conversion call adds cost and failure points."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"In your pipeline, the same Claude Code session that generated a feature is then asked to review its own changes. It rarely finds problems, but human reviewers later catch subtle bugs. What is the best fix?",
o:["Use a separate, independent Claude instance without the generator's reasoning context to review the changes",
"Ask the generating session to review its own changes again with extended thinking enabled for a more careful review",
"Add an instruction to the generating session telling it to be extra critical of its own work during the review step",
"Have the same session review the changes twice and only report issues that it flags consistently in both passes"],
a:[0],e:"A model that retains its generation reasoning is less likely to question its own decisions. An independent review instance without that context catches subtle issues more effectively than self-review instructions or extended thinking in the same session."},

{s:5,d:3,q:"Developers push fixes to a PR, and the CI review re-runs. Each run re-posts many of the same comments, including ones already fixed. How should you address this?",
o:["Include the prior review findings in context and instruct Claude to report only new or still-unaddressed issues",
"Delete all previous review comments on the pull request before each run, so only the newest comments are visible",
"Only run the review on the first commit of each PR, and skip any re-review when new commits are pushed later",
"Lower the review's sensitivity on re-runs so it reports fewer issues, which naturally reduces the duplicate count"],
a:[0],e:"When re-running reviews after new commits, include prior findings in context and instruct Claude to report only new or still-unaddressed issues. Deleting comments loses history; skipping re-review misses new bugs; lowering sensitivity hides real issues."},

{s:5,d:3,q:"Your CI test-generation step keeps proposing tests for scenarios that the existing test suite already covers. What is the most direct fix?",
o:["Provide the existing test files in context so generation avoids scenarios that the current suite already covers",
"Ask Claude to generate twice as many tests, then remove the duplicates afterward by comparing the test names used",
"Tell Claude in the prompt to avoid duplicates, without showing it the tests that are already in the test suite",
"Generate tests only for files that have no tests at all, skipping any file that has partial test coverage today"],
a:[0],e:"Claude can't avoid duplicating what it can't see. Providing existing test files in context lets generation skip covered scenarios. Name comparison misses semantic duplicates; skipping partially covered files leaves gaps."},

{s:5,d:3,q:"Generated tests in CI are low value (testing trivial getters) and ignore your shared fixtures, building test data from scratch each time. Where should you address this so every CI run benefits?",
o:["Document testing standards, what makes a test valuable, and the available fixtures in the project CLAUDE.md",
"Pass a --test-quality=high flag to claude -p, which enables the stricter test generation mode for CI pipelines",
"Increase the number of tests requested per file, so the valuable ones are more likely to appear among the results",
"Add the fixture conventions to each developer's ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md so that they are available on every machine"],
a:[0],e:"CLAUDE.md is the mechanism for giving CI-invoked Claude Code project context: testing standards, valuable-test criteria, and fixture conventions. --test-quality doesn't exist; user-level files aren't present on CI runners."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"Your review prompt says “check that code comments are accurate,” and it produces many false positives (e.g., flagging slightly informal wording). Which replacement instruction most improves precision?",
o:["Flag a comment only when the behavior it claims contradicts the actual behavior of the code it is describing",
"Only report comment issues that you are highly confident about, and be conservative when deciding to flag one",
"Check every comment carefully for accuracy, clarity, grammar, and completeness before reporting any problems",
"Report comment issues only if there are more than three of them in a single file, to reduce the overall noise"],
a:[0],e:"Explicit criteria beat vague instructions: “flag only when claimed behavior contradicts actual code behavior” defines exactly what counts. “Be conservative/high-confidence” instructions don't reliably improve precision; broader checks increase noise."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"Your automated review's “style” category has a 60% false positive rate. Developers have started ignoring all review comments, including accurate security findings. What is the best immediate action?",
o:["Temporarily disable the high false-positive style category to restore trust while the prompt for it is improved",
"Keep all categories enabled but add a disclaimer to each comment noting the review may contain false positives",
"Ask developers to react to each comment so that the model can learn from their feedback during the next review",
"Raise the confidence threshold for all categories, which will reduce the style false positives and keep the rest"],
a:[0],e:"High false-positive categories undermine trust in accurate categories. Temporarily disabling the noisy category restores trust while you improve its prompt. Disclaimers don't fix noise; reactions don't retrain the model; confidence thresholds are a weak precision lever."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"The same kind of issue gets labeled “critical” in one PR and “minor” in another. What is the most effective way to get consistent severity classification?",
o:["Define each severity level with explicit criteria and concrete code examples so classification stays consistent",
"Ask the model to self-report confidence and map high-confidence findings to high severity automatically each time",
"Remove severity entirely and report all findings at one level, so that inconsistent labeling is no longer visible",
"Run each review three times and take the most frequent severity label as the final label for each finding found"],
a:[0],e:"Explicit severity criteria with concrete code examples for each level produce consistent classification. Confidence isn't severity; removing severity loses prioritization; majority voting adds cost without fixing unclear definitions."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"Review comments are posted as a markdown summary. Despite detailed format instructions, some comments omit the file location or a suggested fix, making them hard to act on. What should you add to the prompt?",
o:["Add few-shot examples that show the exact output format with location, issue, severity, and suggested fix fields",
"Add a longer paragraph describing the desired format in more detail, emphasizing that every field is required",
"Post-process the free-text output with a parser that guesses the location from file names mentioned in the text",
"Lower the temperature to zero so that the model produces the same format each time without any extra examples"],
a:[0],e:"Few-shot examples are the most effective technique for consistently formatted, actionable output when detailed instructions alone produce inconsistent results. More prose repeats what isn't working; guessing parsers are brittle."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"The review keeps flagging a deliberate pattern in your codebase (early returns inside guard clauses) as a “control flow issue,” while also catching real bugs. How should you reduce these false positives without losing real findings?",
o:["Add few-shot examples contrasting acceptable code patterns with genuine issues, with the reasoning behind each one",
"Add a list of every acceptable pattern in the codebase so that the model can skip any exact match it encounters",
"Tell the model to skip any pattern that appears more than five times in the codebase, since it is probably fine",
"Remove the review category that produced these false positives, since the model cannot learn to distinguish them"],
a:[0],e:"Few-shot examples distinguishing acceptable patterns from genuine issues reduce false positives while letting the model generalize to novel cases. An exhaustive list only matches exact cases; frequency heuristics would hide repeated bugs."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"Developers dismiss many review findings, and you want to systematically figure out which code constructs trigger the false positives. What change to the structured findings enables this?",
o:["Add a detected_pattern field to each structured finding so dismissed findings can be grouped and analyzed later",
"Ask developers to write a comment explaining why they dismissed each finding, and read those notes each quarter",
"Log the full model transcript for every review so that someone can read through them to find recurring problems",
"Track only the total count of dismissed findings per week, since the overall trend shows whether quality improves"],
a:[0],e:"Adding a detected_pattern field to structured findings lets you track which code constructs trigger findings and analyze dismissal patterns systematically. Free-text notes and transcripts are hard to aggregate; totals lose the “why.”"},

{s:5,d:4,q:"Your team uses Claude for several CI-related workloads. Which TWO are good fits for the Message Batches API? (Select TWO.)",
o:["A nightly job that generates candidate test cases for files changed in the previous day's merged pull requests",
"A weekly audit that scans the whole repository for deprecated API usage and produces a report for the team leads",
"A required pre-merge security check that must finish before developers are allowed to merge their pull requests",
"An interactive code-review assistant that responds to developer questions in pull request comment threads live",
"A pipeline step that calls tools mid-request to fetch file contents and return results within one batch request"],
a:[0,1],e:"Batch processing fits non-blocking, latency-tolerant workloads like nightly test generation and weekly audits. It's inappropriate for blocking pre-merge checks and interactive use (up to 24h, no latency SLA), and it doesn't support multi-turn tool calling within a request."},

{s:5,d:1,q:"After switching to per-file review passes, review depth improved, but a bug was missed: module A passes amounts in cents while module B expects dollars. How should you adjust the review architecture?",
o:["Add a separate integration pass that examines cross-file data flow, in addition to the per-file local review passes",
"Return to a single pass over all files together, since per-file reviews cannot catch issues of any kind across files",
"Increase the per-file review's context to include the full repository, so each file review sees every other file",
"Ask developers to annotate units in comments, so that the per-file review can detect mismatches within single files"],
a:[0],e:"Multi-pass review = per-file local passes plus a separate cross-file integration pass for data flow. Going back to a single pass reintroduces attention dilution; stuffing the repo into every per-file pass defeats its focus."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"You want to auto-post high-confidence review findings and send lower-confidence ones to a human triage queue. What approach supports this routing?",
o:["Have a verification pass output confidence per finding, then calibrate thresholds against labeled examples",
"Post every finding automatically, since developers can dismiss any that are wrong and the cost of noise is small",
"Use the length of each finding's explanation as a proxy for confidence, since longer explanations imply certainty",
"Route all findings to humans for triage, since model confidence can never be used as part of a routing decision"],
a:[0],e:"Running a verification pass where the model self-reports confidence for each finding enables routing—but thresholds should be calibrated against labeled data. Posting everything erodes trust; explanation length isn't a confidence signal."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"Your review prompt already says “Be conservative. Only report findings you are highly confident about.” False positives remain high. What is the best explanation and next step?",
o:["General instructions like “be conservative” fail to improve precision; define which categories to report or skip",
"The model ignores instructions placed in CI prompts, so the criteria must be moved into each developer's settings",
"Conservative instructions only work with extended thinking enabled, so it should be turned on in the CI pipeline",
"The prompt is too short; repeating the conservative instruction several times will make the model follow it more"],
a:[0],e:"General confidence-based instructions don't improve precision the way specific categorical criteria do. Define which issues to report (bugs, security) vs. skip (minor style, local patterns). Repetition and thinking toggles don't fix vague criteria."},

// ---------------- Scenario 1: Customer Support Resolution Agent ----------------
{s:1,d:1,q:"After adding “Always verify the customer with get_customer before any refund” to the system prompt, evaluation shows 99.2% compliance. A teammate says this is good enough. How should you respond?",
o:["No; for financial operations the non-zero failure rate of prompts warrants a programmatic gate before refunds run",
"Yes; 99.2% compliance is excellent for an LLM, and further engineering effort would show diminishing returns overall",
"No; the prompt should be rewritten with stronger emphasis until compliance reaches 100% in the evaluation test set",
"Yes, as long as a weekly audit of refunds identifies the 0.8% of cases where verification was skipped by the agent"],
a:[0],e:"When deterministic compliance is required (identity verification before financial operations), prompt instructions alone have a non-zero failure rate. A programmatic prerequisite that blocks process_refund until verification succeeds provides the guarantee. Audits detect harm after the fact."},

{s:1,d:1,q:"Which statement best describes the difference between hooks and prompt instructions for enforcing business rules in an Agent SDK application?",
o:["Hooks give deterministic guarantees for business rules, while prompts give only probabilistic compliance",
"Prompt instructions and hooks are equally reliable, so the choice depends only on which one is easier to maintain",
"Hooks are probabilistic because the model can decide to skip them, while prompts are enforced by the API itself",
"Hooks should only be used for logging, since blocking tool calls in a hook will cause the agent loop to crash out"],
a:[0],e:"Hooks run in your code, outside the model's discretion, so they provide deterministic guarantees. Prompts influence the model probabilistically. Choose hooks when business rules require guaranteed compliance."},

{s:1,d:1,q:"The API returns a response with stop_reason “tool_use” containing a text block (“Let me check that order for you.”) and a tool_use block for lookup_order. Your loop shows the text to the customer and waits for their reply. What should it do?",
o:["Execute the requested tool, append its tool_result to the history, and call the model again to continue the work",
"Return the text to the customer and wait for them to reply, since the model has already produced a visible message",
"Discard the text block, re-send the same request, and wait for a response that contains no text before continuing",
"Treat the text as the final answer and log the tool request so that a human agent can execute it later if needed"],
a:[0],e:"stop_reason “tool_use” means the model wants a tool executed. Run it, append the tool_result, and continue the loop until “end_turn.” The presence of text is not a completion signal."},

{s:1,d:1,q:"A teammate proposes “stop the agent loop after 5 iterations” as the main way to decide when the agent is done. What is the best evaluation of this proposal?",
o:["Use stop_reason as the primary stop condition; a cap may be a safety net but should not define normal completion",
"Adopt the cap, since five iterations covers most support cases and guarantees that every conversation will end fast",
"Use a cap of 50 instead of 5, since a larger cap removes the risk of cutting off complex cases before they are done",
"Replace the cap with a check for the phrase “Is there anything else,” which signals the agent has finished its work"],
a:[0],e:"Arbitrary iteration caps as the primary stopping mechanism are an anti-pattern: they cut off legitimate work or run too long. Use stop_reason (“end_turn”) to terminate; a cap is at most a safety guard. Phrase parsing is also an anti-pattern."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"You're adding few-shot examples to the system prompt to improve escalation decisions. What should those examples contain to be most effective?",
o:["Contrasting cases showing when to escalate versus resolve, with reasoning for why each choice was made",
"Only examples of cases that were escalated, so the model learns the full range of situations that require humans",
"Only examples of cases resolved autonomously, so the model gains confidence in handling more cases by itself",
"Dozens of examples covering every refund amount, so that the model can match each new case to a nearby example"],
a:[0],e:"Effective few-shot examples (2–4, targeted) show ambiguous cases and the reasoning for choosing one action over plausible alternatives. That lets the model generalize judgment. One-sided examples bias the model; exhaustive coverage encourages pattern matching instead of reasoning."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"A product manager proposes: “Have the agent rate its confidence 1–10 on each case and escalate whenever it's below 7.” What is the main problem with this approach?",
o:["LLM self-reported confidence is poorly calibrated, and the agent is often confidently wrong on the hardest cases",
"Confidence scores are reliable, but a 7/10 threshold is too low and should be raised to 9/10 for better results",
"The agent cannot output numbers reliably, so confidence must be given as words such as low, medium, or high",
"Confidence scores slow down responses so much that the 80% first-contact resolution target becomes unreachable"],
a:[0],e:"Self-reported confidence is an unreliable proxy for case complexity—the agent is often confidently wrong on exactly the hard cases (e.g., policy exceptions). Explicit escalation criteria with few-shot examples address the real problem."},

{s:1,d:1,q:"A customer returns the next day to follow up on yesterday's refund case. Yesterday's session included order status lookups that are now likely outdated. What is the most reliable way to continue?",
o:["Start a new session seeded with a structured summary of the prior case, then re-fetch current order data via tools",
"Resume the prior session as is, since the old tool results remain accurate for at least several days after lookup",
"Resume the prior session and ask the customer to confirm whether anything has changed since yesterday's chat",
"Start a new session with no prior information, and have the customer explain the whole issue again from scratch"],
a:[0],e:"When prior tool results are stale, starting fresh with an injected structured summary is more reliable than resuming with stale data. Re-fetch current state with tools. Making the customer repeat everything hurts the experience."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"The agent receives a long account history (dozens of past tickets) in one tool result. It reliably uses details near the start and end but often misses important details in the middle. What is the best mitigation?",
o:["Place a summary of key facts at the beginning of the input and organize the details under explicit section headers",
"Place the most important details in the middle of the input, since the model reads that region with most attention",
"Shuffle the order of the account history entries on each request so that every entry spends time at the beginning",
"Remove all section headers from the input so that the model reads the history as one continuous block of text"],
a:[0],e:"The “lost in the middle” effect: models reliably process the beginning and end of long inputs but may miss the middle. Put a key-findings summary at the beginning and use explicit section headers to organize details."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"To save tokens, a developer changed the integration to send only the customer's latest message on each API call. The agent now forgets everything discussed earlier in the conversation. What is the correct design?",
o:["Send the complete conversation history with each request, since the API is stateless and keeps no memory of turns",
"Keep sending only the latest message, since the API stores the earlier turns server-side for each conversation ID",
"Send only the first and latest user messages, since those contain the issue and the most recent customer request",
"Move the conversation history into the system prompt as one paragraph and resend only the latest user message"],
a:[0],e:"Maintaining coherence requires passing the complete conversation history in subsequent requests—the Messages API doesn't store turns. Manage size by trimming tool outputs and extracting case facts, not by dropping history."},

{s:1,d:2,q:"get_customer returns HTTP 403 because the agent's service account isn't authorized to access enterprise-tier accounts. What should the MCP tool return to the agent?",
o:["isError true with a permission category and isRetryable false, so the agent routes the case rather than retrying",
"isError true with a transient category and isRetryable true, so the agent tries again after a short wait period",
"isError false with an empty customer record, so the agent asks the customer to confirm their account details",
"isError true with a validation category, so the agent asks the customer to re-enter their account identifier"],
a:[0],e:"This is a permission error: not transient, not a validation problem. Categorize it correctly and mark it non-retryable so the agent escalates/routes appropriately. Returning an empty success suppresses the error and misleads the customer."},

{s:1,d:2,q:"lookup_order is called with “12345” but order IDs must be formatted “ORD-12345”. Which tool response lets the agent recover best?",
o:["A validation error explaining that IDs must match ORD-#####, so the agent can reformat the input or ask the customer",
"A transient error marked retryable, so the agent sends the identical request again until the lookup eventually works",
"An empty result marked successful, so the agent tells the customer that no order exists with the number provided",
"A generic “Operation failed” message, so the agent avoids exposing internal formats to the customer during chat"],
a:[0],e:"Validation errors (invalid input) should say what's wrong so the agent can fix the input. Retrying identical bad input never works; a fake empty success misinforms the customer; generic errors prevent recovery."},

{s:1,d:1,q:"You need to guarantee that process_refund is never executed for amounts over $500, and that the agent is redirected to escalate_to_human instead. Which hook design fits?",
o:["A PreToolUse hook matched to process_refund that denies calls over the limit and redirects the agent to escalation",
"A PostToolUse hook on process_refund that reverses the refund after it is issued if the amount exceeded the limit",
"A Stop hook that reviews the conversation at the end and flags any refunds above the limit for later manual review",
"A UserPromptSubmit hook that rejects customer messages mentioning amounts above the limit before the agent sees them"],
a:[0],e:"Intercepting the outgoing tool call (PreToolUse) blocks the policy-violating action before it happens and can redirect to escalation. PostToolUse runs after execution—too late. Stop-hook flagging is after the fact; filtering customer messages is the wrong layer."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"The agent has been going back and forth for 12 turns on a shipping issue, repeatedly calling the same tools with no new information. The customer hasn't asked for a human. What should happen?",
o:["Escalate, since inability to make meaningful progress is a valid trigger even without a request for a human agent",
"Keep trying, since the customer has not asked for a human and the policy technically covers this type of situation",
"Close the conversation and send the customer an email survey to collect more details about the shipping problem",
"Restart the conversation from scratch, since a fresh context will usually let the agent resolve the issue quickly"],
a:[0],e:"Appropriate escalation triggers are: customer requests for a human, policy exceptions/gaps, and inability to make meaningful progress. Looping without progress is the third trigger."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"A customer is resolving issues with three different orders in one session. The agent starts mixing up which refund amount belongs to which order. What is the best context design?",
o:["Persist structured data for each issue, such as order ID, amount, and status, in a separate context layer",
"Handle all three orders in one combined narrative summary that the model refreshes at the end of every single turn",
"Ask the customer to handle only one order per conversation, so that issue data never gets mixed together again",
"Increase the context window size so that the model can keep all three orders' full tool outputs in its history"],
a:[0],e:"For multi-issue sessions, extract and persist structured issue data (order IDs, amounts, statuses) in a separate context layer included with each prompt. Narrative summaries blur facts together; forcing one order per chat hurts resolution."},

{s:1,d:2,q:"Tool descriptions for get_customer and lookup_order are now detailed and clear, but the agent still calls get_customer first even when given an order number. The system prompt says: “Whenever the customer mentions their account or anything about them, check the account first.” What is most likely happening?",
o:["Keyword-sensitive wording in the system prompt is overriding the descriptions, and that instruction should be revised",
"The lookup_order description is now too long, and long descriptions make the model less likely to select that tool",
"The agent cannot be trusted to choose between the two tools, so get_customer should be removed from the toolset",
"The model needs tool_choice forcing lookup_order on every turn where the customer's message includes a number"],
a:[0],e:"System prompt wording can create unintended tool associations: keyword-sensitive instructions can override well-written descriptions. Review and revise the system prompt. Removing tools or forcing by keyword breaks legitimate flows."},

// ---------------- Scenario 3: Multi-Agent Research System ----------------
{s:3,d:1,q:"After a thorough shared analysis of a research corpus, you want to explore two different report structures (chronological vs. thematic) without one exploration contaminating the other. What should you use?",
o:["Use fork_session to create two independent branches from the shared analysis baseline and explore each separately",
"Run the full analysis twice in two brand-new sessions, so that each structure starts with no shared prior context",
"Continue in a single session and ask the model to alternate between both structures on every other turn it takes",
"Use --resume on the same session twice at once, since resumed sessions automatically become independent copies"],
a:[0],e:"fork_session creates independent branches from a shared baseline to explore divergent approaches without redoing the shared analysis. Rerunning from scratch wastes work; alternating in one session contaminates both explorations."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"You ran a long investigation session named “ai-regulation-eu” yesterday and want to pick up exactly where you left off today. What should you do?",
o:["Use claude --resume with the session's name to continue that specific investigation with its prior conversation",
"Use claude --continue tomorrow, which always reopens the session whose name best matches the current directory",
"Copy the terminal scrollback into a text file and paste it into a new session as the very first user message",
"Use /compact before quitting, since compacted sessions are automatically restored the next time Claude starts"],
a:[0],e:"Named session resumption with --resume <session-name> continues a specific prior conversation. --continue resumes the most recent session, not one matched by name; manual pasting loses structure; /compact doesn't auto-restore."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"A multi-hour research pipeline crashes when the analysis subagent is halfway through. Restarting loses all prior progress. What design enables reliable recovery?",
o:["Have each agent export its state to a known location and the coordinator load a manifest on resume to re-inject it",
"Keep all state in the coordinator's conversation history, since it is automatically persisted across process crashes",
"Restart the pipeline from the beginning after any crash, since partial state is too risky to reuse in a new run",
"Have each subagent keep its state in memory and resume where it left off, since subagents share memory between runs"],
a:[0],e:"Structured state persistence for crash recovery: each agent exports state to a known location, and the coordinator loads a manifest on resume and injects it into agent prompts. Subagents don't share memory between runs."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"The synthesis subagent keeps running out of context because the analysis subagent returns long reasoning chains and full-text excerpts for every document. What is the best fix?",
o:["Modify the analysis agent to return structured key facts, citations, and relevance scores, not verbose reasoning",
"Give the synthesis agent a larger model with a bigger context window so it can absorb the verbose reasoning chains",
"Truncate the analysis agent's output at a fixed character limit so that it always fits within the synthesis budget",
"Have the synthesis agent skim only the first paragraph of each analysis, since key points are usually stated early"],
a:[0],e:"When downstream agents have limited context budgets, modify upstream agents to return structured data (key facts, citations, relevance scores) instead of verbose content and reasoning chains. Truncation drops arbitrary information."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"The coordinator concatenates eight subagent reports and passes them to synthesis. Findings from the reports in positions 3–6 are frequently omitted from the final report. What is the best mitigation?",
o:["Lead the aggregated input with a key findings summary and organize the detailed results under explicit section headers",
"Concatenate the subagent reports in random order each run so that no single report is consistently placed in the middle",
"Ask the synthesis agent to read the input twice, since a second reading reliably recovers the findings from the middle",
"Limit the number of subagents to two, so that there is never a middle report to be overlooked during the synthesis"],
a:[0],e:"This is the lost-in-the-middle effect. Place key findings summaries at the beginning of aggregated inputs and use explicit section headers to mitigate position effects. Randomizing just moves the problem around."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"Final reports contain accurate claims, but many lack citations, even though the search subagent found the sources. Investigation shows attribution is lost in the analysis and synthesis summarization steps. What is the best fix?",
o:["Require structured claim-source mappings from subagents and require synthesis to preserve and merge those mappings",
"Add a final citation agent that searches the web again to find plausible sources for each claim in the final report",
"Ask the report agent to add a bibliography of all sources consulted, without linking specific claims to the sources",
"Remove summarization steps entirely and pass every raw document directly through to the final report generator"],
a:[0],e:"Source attribution is lost when findings are compressed without claim-source mappings. Require structured mappings (source URL, document name, excerpt) and have synthesis preserve and merge them. Post-hoc citation hunting can attach wrong sources."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"A research report covers a company's quarterly revenue, recent news coverage, and technical details of its products. The synthesis agent formats everything as identical bullet lists. What is the better approach?",
o:["Render each content type appropriately, such as financial data as tables, news as prose, and tech findings as lists",
"Convert all content into bullet points so that the report looks consistent and readers can scan it more quickly",
"Convert all content into prose paragraphs so that the report reads as a single narrative from beginning to end",
"Convert all content into tables, since tables are the most precise format for every kind of research finding"],
a:[0],e:"Different content types should be rendered appropriately in synthesis—financial data as tables, news as prose, technical findings as structured lists—rather than converted to one uniform format."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"Sources broadly agree on some findings about a drug's efficacy but disagree on its long-term side effects. How should the final report be structured?",
o:["Separate well-established findings from contested ones, preserving source characterizations and methodological context",
"Report only findings that all sources agree on, dropping contested claims so the report does not confuse its readers",
"Merge established and contested findings together and let the reader determine which claims are reliable on their own",
"Rank findings by the number of sources mentioning them and present the top ten as the established findings of record"],
a:[0],e:"Reports should have explicit sections distinguishing well-established from contested findings, preserving original source characterizations and methodological context. Dropping contested findings hides important uncertainty."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"While analyzing documents, the analysis subagent finds that two credible reports give different values for the same statistic. What should the analysis subagent do?",
o:["Complete the analysis with both values included and annotated, letting the coordinator decide how to reconcile them",
"Have the analysis agent pick the value from the more prestigious source, so that the coordinator receives one number",
"Stop the analysis as soon as a conflict is found and return an error, so the coordinator can restart the analysis",
"Pass the conflict straight to the report agent, which is best positioned to choose a value for the final document"],
a:[0],e:"Complete the analysis with conflicting values included and explicitly annotated, and let the coordinator decide how to reconcile before passing to synthesis. The analysis agent shouldn't silently pick; bypassing the coordinator breaks hub-and-spoke control."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"The synthesis agent describes a 40-person pilot study as if it were a large clinical trial, overstating the strength of the evidence. What is the best fix?",
o:["Require subagents to include methodological context, dates, and source locations in their structured outputs",
"Ask the synthesis agent to assume that every study is a pilot study unless the text explicitly says otherwise first",
"Exclude all pilot studies from the research corpus so that their results can never be misrepresented in a report",
"Have the report agent add a generic disclaimer stating that some studies cited may have used smaller sample sizes"],
a:[0],e:"Require subagents to include metadata (dates, source locations, methodological context like sample size and study type) in structured outputs to support accurate downstream synthesis. Blanket assumptions and disclaimers don't fix the information gap."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"When configuring a subagent type with an AgentDefinition in the Agent SDK, what should the definition specify?",
o:["A description for when to use it, a system prompt for its role, and a restricted tool list for its specialty",
"Only a name, since the coordinator writes every subagent's instructions dynamically at the moment of invocation",
"A shared conversation history, since each subagent must inherit the coordinator's context to perform its role",
"A list of the other subagents it may message directly, since subagents coordinate their work among themselves"],
a:[0],e:"AgentDefinition includes a description (used for delegation decisions), a system prompt, and tool restrictions for each subagent type. Subagents don't inherit history, and communication routes through the coordinator."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"The research system receives the task: “Investigate why enterprise customer churn rose last quarter.” The causes are unknown at the start. Which decomposition pattern fits best?",
o:["Dynamic decomposition that generates new subtasks based on what each step discovers, adapting the plan as it goes",
"A fixed sequential prompt chain defined up front, so every investigation follows exactly the same predefined steps",
"A single prompt asking for the complete answer at once, since decomposition adds coordination overhead to the run",
"Parallel subagents assigned random subtopics, so that the investigation covers a broad and unbiased mix of areas"],
a:[0],e:"Open-ended investigations call for dynamic, adaptive decomposition that generates subtasks based on intermediate findings. Fixed prompt chains suit predictable multi-aspect work (like structured reviews)."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"For niche topics, the web search subagent legitimately finds no results. The coordinator treats every empty result as a failure and retries five times, wasting minutes. What is the right fix?",
o:["Have the subagent distinguish valid empty results from access failures, so only real failures get retried",
"Have the coordinator retry every empty result ten times, since search engines are inconsistent across requests",
"Have the subagent return an error whenever results are empty, so that the coordinator always tries other sources",
"Have the subagent pad empty results with loosely related sources, so the coordinator always has something to use"],
a:[0],e:"Valid empty results (successful query, no matches) and access failures (timeouts, outages) need different handling. Make the distinction explicit in reporting so the coordinator can decide appropriately. Padding with irrelevant sources degrades quality."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"The web search subagent times out on a query. Which report to the coordinator best enables intelligent recovery?",
o:["{failure: timeout, query: “EU AI Act fines 2025”, partial: 3 results, alternatives: [news API, narrower query]}",
"{status: “search unavailable”} returned once all of the retries inside the subagent have been fully exhausted",
"{status: “success”, results: []} so that the coordinator can continue working without interruption on the task",
"An unhandled exception thrown to the top-level handler, which then stops the entire research workflow at once"],
a:[0],e:"Structured error context—failure type, attempted query, partial results, potential alternatives—lets the coordinator retry differently, try alternatives, or proceed with partial results. Generic statuses hide context; fake success suppresses the error; crashing the workflow is disproportionate."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"Which TWO statements correctly describe how subagents receive context in the Claude Agent SDK? (Select TWO.)",
o:["Subagents start with isolated context and do not automatically inherit the coordinator's conversation history",
"Anything a subagent needs, such as prior findings, must be passed explicitly in the prompt it receives via Task",
"Subagents automatically share a memory store, so findings from one invocation are visible in the next invocation",
"Subagents inherit the coordinator's full history by default, but this can be disabled with an AgentDefinition flag",
"Subagents read the coordinator's scratchpad automatically, so findings written there never need to be passed on"],
a:[0,1],e:"Subagents operate with isolated context: they don't inherit the coordinator's history or share memory between invocations. Required context must be explicitly included in the prompt passed via the Task tool."}
  ]
});
